.TH DWMR 1 dwmr\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dwmr \- dynamic window manager (Rust port of dwm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwmr
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
dwmr is a dynamic window manager for X, a port of dwm to Rust with the same behaviour. It manages windows in tiled (spiral, tile,
bottom stack, dwindle, deck, centered master and centered floating master),
monocle and floating layouts. Either layout can be applied dynamically, optimising the
environment for the application in use and the task performed.
.P
In tiled layouts windows are managed in a master and stacking area. The master
area on the left contains one window by default, and the stacking area on the
right contains all other windows. The number of master area windows can be
adjusted from zero to an arbitrary number. In monocle layout all windows are
maximised to the screen size. In floating layout windows can be resized and
moved freely. Dialog windows are always managed floating, regardless of the
layout applied.
.P
The tiled layouts leave gaps between the windows and between the windows and
the screen edge (vanitygaps). The default layout is the Fibonacci spiral.
When the only tiled window on a screen is Firefox (its class starts with
\(lqfirefox\(rq, in any case), it gets no outer gaps unless
.B browsergaps
is enabled; with
.B smartgaps = true
no single window gets outer gaps.
.P
Windows are grouped by tags. Each window can be tagged with one or multiple
tags. Selecting certain tags displays all windows with these tags.
.P
Each screen contains a small status bar which displays the tags in use, the
layout, and the text read from the root window name property (on every
screen, not only the focused one). The title of the focused window is not shown (notitle), so the bar
does not change when a window renames itself. Only tags that are selected or
have a window are shown; vacant tags take no space (hide_vacant_tags). A
window tagged on every tag does not make a tag count as occupied. The
selected tags are indicated with a different color; there are no occupancy
squares in the tag corners.
.P
dwmr draws a small border around windows to indicate the focus state. The
border is omitted when it would carry no information: for the only tiled
window on a screen and for every window in the monocle layout (noborder).
Focus follows mouse clicks only, never pointer movement (focusonclick). By
default the scroll wheel (Button4 and Button5) does not change the focus or
the selected screen either; set
.B focusonwheel = true
in the configuration file to make it do so.
.P
A window started from a terminal swallows it: it takes the terminal's place
in the layout, and the terminal reappears when the window is closed (swallow).
Rules mark terminals with
.B isterminal = true
and exempt windows with
.BR "noswallow = true" ;
floating windows only swallow with
.BR "swallowfloating = true" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.SH USAGE
.SS Status bar
.TP
.B X root window name
is read and displayed in the status text area. It can be set with the
.BR xsetroot (1)
command. Up to 1023 bytes are shown. Codes of the form
.B ^...^
are not drawn but change how the following text is drawn (dwm's status2d
patch, colour codes only): the text starts in
.BR col1 ;
.BR ^3^ ,
.BR ^4^ ,
.B ^5^
and
.B ^6^
switch to
.B col3
to
.BR col6 ,
.B ^c#rrggbb^
to any colour, and
.B ^2^
to the weather colour chosen from the temperature that follows it: +20 and
above
.BR col21 ,
below +20
.BR col22 ,
negative
.BR col23 ,
no sign
.BR col24
(all set in
.BR statuscolors ).
.B ^B^
switches to the bigger
.B statusbigfonts
until
.BR ^N^ ,
e.g. for a block's icon. Other codes are ignored and an unterminated
.B ^
ends the text.
.IP
On startup dwmr kills a running
.B statusbar
program (default dwmblocksr) and starts it again;
.B statusbar = \(dq\(dq
starts none.
.IP
Bytes below 32 are not drawn: status bars like dwmblocksr put a block's
signal as such a byte before each block, to make the blocks clickable
(dwm's statuscmd patch).
.TP
.B Button1/Button2/Button3
click on a status block sends the block's signal to the status bar program
.B statusbar
(default dwmblocksr) as SIGRTMIN+signal, with the button (1, 2 or 3) as the
.BR sigqueue (3)
value; the program then runs the block's command with
.B BLOCK_BUTTON
set to it
.RB ( sigstatusbar ).
.TP
.B Button1
click on a tag label to display all windows with that tag, click on the layout
label toggles between the current and the previous layout.
.TP
.B Button3
click on a tag label adds/removes all windows with that tag to/from the view.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button1
click on a tag label applies that tag to the focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button3
click on a tag label adds/removes that tag to/from the focused window.
.TP
.B Button4/Button5
scrolling on the tag bar views the previous/next tag (shift-tools).
.SS Keyboard commands
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-Return
Start
.BR st(1).
.TP
.B Mod1\-p
Spawn
.BR dmenu(1)
for launching other programs.
.TP
.B Mod1\-,
Focus previous screen, if any.
.TP
.B Mod1\-.
Focus next screen, if any.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-,
Send focused window to previous screen, if any.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-.
Send focused window to next screen, if any.
.TP
.B Mod1\-'
Show or hide the first scratchpad window, starting its command if there is
none yet (scratchpads).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-'
Show or hide the second scratchpad window, starting its command if there is
none yet (scratchpads).
.TP
.B Mod1\-b
Toggles bar on and off.
.TP
.B Mod1\-t
Sets tiled layout.
.TP
.B Mod1\-f
Sets floating layout.
.TP
.B Mod1\-m
Sets monocle layout.
.TP
.B Mod1\-space
Toggles between current and previous layout.
.TP
.B Mod1\-j
Focus next window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-k
Focus previous window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Control\-j
Focus the last window of the stack (stacker).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Control\-k
Focus the first window of the stack (stacker).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-j
Move focused window one position down the stack (stacker).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-k
Move focused window one position up the stack (stacker).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-Control\-j
Move focused window to the bottom of the stack (stacker).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-Control\-k
Move focused window to the top of the stack (stacker).
.TP
.B Mod1\-i
Increase number of windows in master area.
.TP
.B Mod1\-d
Decrease number of windows in master area.
.TP
.B Mod1\-l
Increase master area size.
.TP
.B Mod1\-h
Decrease master area size.
.TP
.B Mod1\-x
Reset the gaps of the focused screen to the configured ones (vanitygaps).
.TP
.B Mod1\-z
Toggle the gaps on and off.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Control\-z
Toggle the outer gaps of a lone Firefox window (browsergaps).
.TP
.B Mod1\-plus / Mod1\-minus
Increase/decrease all gaps of the focused screen by 3 pixels.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-plus / Mod1\-Shift\-minus
Increase/decrease all gaps of the focused screen by 1 pixel.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Return
Zooms/cycles focused window to/from master area (tiled layouts only).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-c
Close focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-f
Toggle fullscreen for focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-<
Toggle sticky state for focused window: a sticky window is shown on every
tag (sticky).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-space
Toggle focused window between tiled and floating state. A window returns to
the geometry it last floated with; one that never floated, or whose saved
geometry lies on another monitor, is centred on its monitor (savefloats,
togglefloatingcenter).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Tab
View the next tag that has a window, or the next tag if none has one
(shift-tools).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-Tab
View the previous tag that has a window, or the previous tag if none has one
(shift-tools).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-y
Move focused window to the next tag (shift-tools).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-o
Move focused window to the previous tag (shift-tools).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-[1..n]
Apply nth tag to focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-0
Apply all tags to focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Control\-Shift\-[1..n]
Add/remove nth tag to/from focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-[1..n]
View all windows with nth tag.
.TP
.B Mod1\-0
View all windows with any tag.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Control\-[1..n]
Add/remove all windows with nth tag to/from the view.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-q
Quit dwmr.
.SS Mouse commands
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button1
Move focused window while dragging. Tiled windows will be toggled to the floating state.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button2
Reset the gaps to the configured ones (vanitygaps).
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button3
Resize focused window while dragging. Tiled windows will be toggled to the floating state.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Button4 / Mod1\-Button5
Increase/decrease all gaps by 1 pixel.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
dwmr is customized through ~/.config/dwmr/config.toml (see CONFIGURATION below); it
also builds in the same defaults as dwm, so the file is optional.
.SH CONFIGURATION
On startup dwmr reads
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dwmr/config.toml
(usually
.BR ~/.config/dwmr/config.toml ).
The file mirrors dwm's config.def.h: appearance, colors, tags, rules, layouts,
the modifier key, commands, key bindings and mouse buttons. Every key is
optional and falls back to the built-in default, which is dwm's default
configuration. If the file cannot be parsed the error is printed on stderr and
the defaults are used, so the window manager always starts. A default file is
installed with
.B make install-config
and shipped as config/config.toml in the source tree.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dwm (1),
.BR dmenu (1),
.BR st (1)
.SH ISSUES
Java applications which use the XToolkit/XAWT backend may draw grey windows
only. The XToolkit/XAWT backend breaks ICCCM-compliance in recent JDK 1.5 and early
JDK 1.6 versions, because it assumes a reparenting window manager. Possible workarounds
are using JDK 1.4 (which doesn't contain the XToolkit/XAWT backend) or setting the
environment variable
.BR AWT_TOOLKIT=MToolkit
(to use the older Motif backend instead) or running
.B xprop -root -f _NET_WM_NAME 32a -set _NET_WM_NAME LG3D
or
.B wmname LG3D
(to pretend that a non-reparenting window manager is running that the
XToolkit/XAWT backend can recognize) or when using OpenJDK setting the environment variable
.BR _JAVA_AWT_WM_NONREPARENTING=1 .
.SH BUGS
Send all bug reports with a patch to hackers@suckless.org.
